PHASE-SYNC / GPU-STARVATION ANSWER (the key question):
YES, the workers phase-synchronize and starve the GPU - but it's a STARTUP TRANSIENT, not permanent. When N workers claim fresh volumes together they ALL enter CPU-bound STAGE1-RENDER+STAGE2-PREPROCESS in lockstep -> CPU saturates -> GPU sits idle (5090 GPU util MEDIAN 8% even across the 'OCR window' because 3 of 4 workers were still prepping most of the time). At 4 workers the lockstep prep is WORSE (orig agent measured per-worker prep collapse ~50->5 pg/min under 4-way CPU contention; GPU idle 0-3% for the first ~70 min). The 5080's 2 workers actually kept its GPU BUSIER (util median 19%) than the 5090's 4 workers kept the 5090 GPU (median 8%) - direct evidence the 5090's extra workers do NOT convert to GPU OCR; they convert to CPU-prep contention. Workers DO eventually desync (uneven volume sizes + supervisor restarts) and overlap their OCR phases, which is when the ~70 pg/min was observed. CONCLUSION: the 4th 5090 worker is net CPU-prep contention with little GPU benefit; the 2nd 5080 worker actively HARMS quality (Surya OOM). The pipeline is CPU-prep-bound at the front and the 5080 is VRAM-bound for concurrent Surya.

HANG: NO true hang. One FALSE hang-suspect at 18:56 (CPU%-counter transient near-0 mid-prep) - corrected the monitor to use worker-log page-advancement as the authoritative liveness signal (see FINDING above). All bumps/kills/drains clean.

RECOMMENDATION: Stay at 3/1 (current). To safely exploit more hardware: (a) NEVER run 2 concurrent Surya workers on the 16GB 5080 - it OOMs Surya; 1 is the ceiling there. (b) The 5090 (32GB) is GPU-underutilized but is gated by single-threaded-feeling CPU prep lockstep; a real 4-worker win needs decoupling CPU prep from GPU OCR (separate prep pool feeding a GPU OCR pool) or pre-rendering volumes, not just adding a worker. As-is the 4th 5090 worker and 2nd 5080 worker are not worth it.

